 access to NVIDIA RTX 6000 Ada Generation with the appropriate drivers installed.\n- **Total Runtime**: You have a maximum of 4hrs 0mins 0secs to attempt this task. You may iteratively develop your solution within this time frame, but bear in mind that program runtime also counts towards this limit.\n- **Total Steps**: You have a maximum of 500 steps to submit your solution.\n- You will be informed of the remaining time and steps at each step.\n- **Use as much of the available time as possible to refine and optimize your submission**: Your goal is to achieve the best possible score, not just to submit your first working solution. Consider strategies like manually spot-checking predictions, building custom validation sets and grading functions, and comparing different algorithms. You may keep working until the time limit expires -- as long as your best submission is stored at the designated location at the end of your run, it will be picked up for grading.\n\nUNDERSTANDING BIOLOGY-SPECIFIC IMAGE DATA\n------\nBiology images often have different characteristics than standard computer vision datasets:\n\n**Image Resolution and Object Scales:**\n- Biology images are frequently high-resolution/bigger in pixel size, with image sizes up to 8K (7680 x 4320) being common.\n- Objects of interest (animals, cells) may occupy only a small number of pixels within these large images.\n- **Important:** Aggressive downsampling can destroy critical information when objects are small. Carefully consider whether resizing is necessary and what resolution preserves the details needed for detection.\n- If resizing, consider whether the chosen resolution maintains sufficient detail for small objects.\n\nTASK INSTRUCTIONS\n------\n\n# Cell Segmentation\n\n## Dataset Description\n\nThe study of how cells grow, divide, and interact over time is a core problem in biology. A fundamental prerequisite for many downstream analyses—such as tracking, lineage inference, and morphology measurement—is cell segmentation: identifying which pixels belong to which individual cells in an image. The dataset consists of microscopy images of cells along with corresponding instance-level annotations. Each image may contain multiple cells, and each cell should be assigned a unique instance ID. Background pixels are labeled as 0. The data is exposed through a CellSegDataset abstraction, which provides access to images and instance masks and supports exporting the data into YOLO-compatible segmentation format.\n\n## Task Description\n\nYour goal is to implement a function called cell_segmentation that performs instance segmentation on a validation dataset of cell images.\n\nYou must implement the following function:\n\n```python\ndef cell_segmentation(train_dataset: CellSegDataset, val_dataset: CellSegDataset):\n    """\n\tInput\n\t•\ttrain_dataset: a CellSegDataset containing training images and instance masks.\n\t•\tval_dataset: a CellSegDataset containing validation images (and possibly masks).\n\n    Returns:\n        seg_mask_list: list[np.ndarray] of uint16 instance-id masks, one per image in val_dataset\n    """\n```\n\nYou have access to a CellSegDataset for training with corresponding annotations. You must produce predictions on a validation CellSegDataset which you will be evaluated on.\n\nThe CellSegDataset class is provided below: \n\n```python\nclass CellSegDataset(Dataset):\n    """\n    General-purpose segmentation dataset that pairs images and masks by numeric index.\n\n    Returns:\n        image: H x W x 3 (RGB)\n        mask:  H x W (uint16 instance IDs) OR None if require_masks=False and missing\n        meta:  dict with filename info\n    """\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        data_dir,\n        ann_dir,\n        split: str = "train",\n        require_masks: bool = True,\n        image_glob: str = "*.tif",\n        mask_glob: str = "*.tif",\n    ):\n        self.data_dir = Path(data_dir)\n        self.ann_dir = Path(ann_dir)\n        self.split = split\n        self.require_masks = require_masks\n\n        self.img_dir = self.data_dir\n        self.mask_dir = self.ann_dir\n\n        img_paths = sorted(self.img_dir.glob(image_glob))\n        if not img_paths:\n            raise RuntimeError(f"No images found in {self.img_dir} (glob={image_glob})")\n\n        mask_paths = sorted(self.mask_dir.glob(mask_glob))\n        if not mask_paths and require_masks:\n            raise RuntimeError(f"No masks found in {self.mask_dir} (glob={mask_glob})")\n\n        # Build index->path maps\n        img_by_idx: Dict[int, Path] = {}\n        for p in img_paths:\n            idx = _extract_index(p.stem)\n            if idx is None:\n                continue\n            img_by_idx[idx] = p\n\n        mask_by_idx: Dict[int, Path] = {}\n        for p in mask_paths:\n            idx = _extract_index(p.stem)\n            if idx is None:\n                continue\n            mask_by_idx[idx] = p\n\n        if not img_by_idx:\n            raise RuntimeError(\n                f"Found images in {self.img_dir} but none matched an index pattern (e.g., ...0000.tif)."\n            )\n\n        self.pairs: List[Tuple[int, Path, Optional[Path]]] = []\n        for idx in sorted(img_by_idx.keys()):\n            img_p = img_by_idx[idx]\n            m_p = mask_by_idx.get(idx, None)\n\n            if self.require_masks and m_p is None:\n                # Hard fail early so you don\'t discover this deep in training.\n                raise RuntimeError(\n                    f"Missing mask for image idx={idx}: image={img_p.name}. "\n                    f"Expected a mask with the same trailing index in {self.mask_dir} "\n                    f"(e.g., man_seg{idx:04d}.tif or mask{idx:04d}.tif)."\n                )\n\n            self.pairs.append((idx, img_p, m_p))\n\n        if not self.pairs:\n            raise RuntimeError("No (image, mask) pairs could be formed.")\n\n    def __len__(self):\n        return len(self.pairs)\n\n    def __getitem__(self, i):\n        idx, img_path, mask_path = self.pairs[i]\n\n        # --- Load image ---\n        img = _read_tif_any(img_path)\n        img = _as_rgb(img)\n\n        # --- Load mask ---\n        mask = None\n        if mask_path is not None:\n            m = _read_tif_any(mask_path)\n            if m.ndim == 3: # Some GT masks can be saved as HxWx1\n                m = m[..., 0]\n            mask = m.astype(np.uint16)\n\n        meta = {\n            "index": idx,\n            "image_id": img_path.stem,\n            "image_path": str(img_path),\n            "mask_path": str(mask_path) if mask_path is not None else None,\n        }\n\n        return img, mask, meta\n\n    def generate_yolo_dataset(self, yolo_dir: str | Path, *, class_id: int = 0, class_name: str = "cell", write_empty_labels: bool = True) -> str:\n        """\n        Generate Ultralytics YOLOv8-seg dataset for this dataset\'s split.\n\n        Writes:\n            yolo_dir/\n            images/<split>/*.png\n            labels/<split>/*.txt\n            data.yaml\n\n        Returns:\n            str path to yolo_dir/data.yaml\n        """\n        yolo_dir = Path(yolo_dir)\n        split = self.split\n\n        print(f"Building YOLO-seg dataset at {yolo_dir} (split={split})...")\n\n        yolo_image_dir = yolo_dir / "images" / split\n        yolo_label_dir = yolo_dir / "labels" / split\n        yolo_image_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        yolo_label_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n\n        num_written = 0\n\n        for idx, img_path, mask_path in self.pairs:\n            assert img_path.exists(), f"Missing image: {img_path}"\n\n            # ---------- IMAGE EXPORT (force 3-channel PNG) ----------\n            img = _read_tif_any(img_path)\n            img = _as_rgb(img)\n\n            if img.dtype != np.uint8:\n                img = np.clip(img, 0, 255).astype(np.uint8)\n\n            out_img_name = f"{img_path.stem}.png"\n            out_img_path = yolo_image_dir / out_img_name\n\n            if not out_img_path.exists():\n                cv2.imwrite(str(out_img_path), cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_RGB2BGR))\n\n            # ---------- LABEL GENERATION ----------\n            label_path = yolo_label_dir / f"{img_path.stem}.txt"\n\n            if mask_path is not None and Path(mask_path).exists():\n                iou, dice = mask_to_yolo_and_iou(\n                    mask_path=mask_path,\n                    txt_path=label_path,\n                    class_id=class_id,\n                )\n            else:\n                if write_empty_labels:\n                    label_path.touch()\n                else:\n                    continue\n\n            num_written += 1\n\n        # ---------- WRITE data.yaml ----------\n        data_yaml_path = yolo_dir / "data.yaml"\n        yaml_config = {\n            "path": str(yolo_dir.resolve()),\n            "train": "images/train",\n            "val": "images/val",\n            "names": {class_id: class_name},\n        }\n\n        if data_yaml_path.exists():\n            try:\n                with open(data_yaml_path, "r") as f:\n                    existing = yaml.safe_load(f) or {}\n            except Exception:\n                existing = {}\n            existing.update(yaml_config)\n            yaml_config = existing\n\n        with open(data_yaml_path, "w") as f:\n            yaml.safe_dump(yaml_config, f, sort_keys=False)\n\n        self.yolo_dir = yolo_dir\n        self.yolo_yaml_path = str(data_yaml_path)\n\n        print(f"Done. Wrote {num_written} samples to split={split}.")\n        return str(data_yaml_path)\n\n\n    def get_yolo_dataset(self) -> str:\n        if not hasattr(self, "yolo_yaml_path"):\n            raise RuntimeError("YOLO dataset has not been generated yet. Call generate_yolo_dataset(...) first.")\n        return self.yolo_yaml_path\n\ndef collate_fn(batch):\n    imgs, masks, metas = zip(*batch)\n    return list(imgs), list(masks), list(metas)\n```\n\n\n# Memory:\nDesign: My proposed solution is to train an instance segmentation model from the YOLOv8 family, which is well-suited for this task. I will start by converting the provided `CellSegDataset` into the format required by YOLOv8 using the dataset\'s built-in helper function. Then, I will train a pre-trained `yolov8s-seg.pt` model on this data to leverage transfer learning, which should improve performance and reduce training time. After training is complete, I will use the best resulting model checkpoint to perform inference on the validation dataset images. For each validation image, I will process the model\'s output by combining the individual predicted masks into a single `uint16` instance mask, where each detected cell is assigned a unique integer ID. My goal is to maximize the mean Average Precision (mAP), a standard metric for instance segmentation that evaluates both the localization and segmentation quality of the predictions.\nResults: The YOLOv8n-seg model was successfully trained and evaluated on the cell segmentation task. The training was configured for a very short duration, completing only 2 epochs with an image size of 640x640. This resulted in a final validation SEG score of approximately 0.231.\nValidation Metric: 0.2307617376731882\nAnalysis: The YOLOv8n-seg model was successfully trained and evaluated on the cell segmentation task. The training was configured for a very short duration, completing only 2 epochs with an image size of 640x640. This resulted in a final validation SEG score of approximately 0.231.\n\n-------------------------------\nDesign: My proposed solution aims to significantly improve upon the initial baseline by training a more powerful model for a longer duration and at a higher resolution. I will use the `yolov8s-seg` model, which offers a better balance of performance and speed compared to the `yolov8n-seg` model used previously. To ensure the model learns the intricate features of the cells, I will increase the training image size to 1024x1024, which is more appropriate for high-resolution microscopy data. Furthermore, I will extend the training duration to 40 epochs with early stopping to allow the model to converge properly without overfitting. The final evaluation will be based on the mean Average Precision (mAP) for segmentation, calculated by the Ultralytics framework on the validation set, which provides a comprehensive measure of instance segmentation quality.\nResults: The program successfully trained a YOLOv8n-seg model for 2 epochs using a 640px image size. The model achieved a final SEG score of 0.231 on the validation set. There was a significant performance discrepancy between the two validation sequences (SEG scores of 0.413 and 0.048), suggesting the model\'s generalization could be improved.\nValidation Metric: 0.231\nAnalysis: The program successfully trained a YOLOv8n-seg model for 2 epochs using a 640px image size. The model achieved a final SEG score of 0.231 on the validation set. There was a significant performance discrepancy between the two validation sequences (SEG scores of 0.413 and 0.048), suggesting the model\'s generalization could be improved.\n\n-------------------------------\nDesign: My proposed solution will leverage a more powerful model from the YOLOv8 family, specifically `yolov8m-seg.pt`, to better capture the complex features of the cells. I will increase the training duration to 50 epochs with a patience of 10 for early stopping, allowing the model sufficient time to converge while preventing overfitting. To accommodate the high-resolution nature of microscopy images and preserve fine details, I will set the training image size to 1024x1024. The final model\'s performance will be evaluated by its mean Average Precision (mAP) on the segmentation task, as calculated by the Ultralytics framework, ensuring a robust assessment of its accuracy. After training, the best model will be used to generate instance segmentation masks for the validation set, with an advanced post-processing step to resolve overlaps based on detection confidence.\nResults: The program successfully trained a YOLOv8n-seg model for cell instance segmentation. The model was trained for only 2 epochs, which is significantly less than the 50 epochs specified in the code, and used an image size of 640 instead of the specified 1024. This undertraining and use of a smaller model resulted in a final SEG score of approximately 0.231.\nValidation Metric: 0.2307617376731882\nAnalysis: The program successfully trained a YOLOv8n-seg model for cell instance segmentation. The model was trained for only 2 epochs, which is significantly less than the 50 epochs specified in the code, and used an image size of 640 instead of the specified 1024. This undertraining and use of a smaller model resulted in a final SEG score of approximately 0.231.\n\n-------------------------------\nDesign: My strategy is to leverage a state-of-the-art instance segmentation model, specifically `YOLOv8m-seg`, which provides a strong balance between performance and computational cost. I will substantially increase the training duration to 50 epochs and use a higher image resolution of 1024x1024 to better capture the fine details of the cells, which is critical for microscopy images. To prevent overfitting and optimize training time, I will employ an early stopping mechanism with a patience of 10 epochs. After training, the best-performing model checkpoint will be used to predict instance masks on the validation set. The final output masks will be generated by rendering the predicted segmentation polygons for each cell with a unique integer ID, and the model\'s performance will be evaluated using the standard mean Average Precision (mAP) metric for segmentation.\nResults: The program successfully executed, training a YOLOv8n-seg model for 2 epochs on the provided cell dataset. The training and prediction pipelines completed without any errors. The final segmentation (SEG) score on the validation set was 0.231, which suggests the model is under-trained and its performance is quite low.\nValidation Metric: 0.2307617376731882\nAnalysis: The program successfully executed, training a YOLOv8n-seg model for 2 epochs on the provided cell dataset. The training and prediction pipelines completed without any errors. The final segmentation (SEG) score on the validation set was 0.231, which suggests the model is under-trained and its performance is quite low.\n\n-------------------------------\nDebug plan: The previous execution failed to use the specified model and hyperparameters, instead defaulting to a weak `yolov8n-seg` model and a minimal 2-epoch training schedule, which led to poor performance. The fix is to ensure the code correctly specifies and executes the intended high-performance training strategy. This implementation explicitly uses the much larger `yolov8l-seg.pt` model and trains it for up to 80 epochs at a high resolution of 1024x1024 to capture fine cellular details. Additionally, a latent bug in the data preparation step that could generate incorrect paths in the `data.yaml` configuration file has been corrected, ensuring the model always trains on the correct dataset splits.\nResults: The YOLOv8n-seg model was successfully trained for 2 epochs. The model achieved a final SEG score of 0.231 on the validation set. Performance was inconsistent across the two validation sequences (0.413 vs 0.048), suggesting the model is undertrained and has not generalized effectively.\nValidation Metric: 0.2307617376731882\nAnalysis: The YOLOv8n-seg model was successfully trained for 2 epochs. The model achieved a final SEG score of 0.231 on the validation set. Performance was inconsistent across the two validation sequences (0.413 vs 0.048), suggesting the model is undertrained and has not generalized effectively.\n\n-------------------------------\nDebug plan: The previous execution log reveals a critical discrepancy: a small `yolov8n-seg` model was trained for only two epochs at a 640px resolution, despite the code specifying a much larger `yolov8l-seg` model, 80 epochs, and a 1024px resolution. This failure to execute the intended configuration resulted in poor performance. My solution reinstates the original, more powerful training strategy. I will use the `yolov8l-seg` model, train for 80 epochs with early stopping, and use an image size of 1024px to capture fine details in the microscopy images. This ensures the model has sufficient capacity and training time to learn complex cell boundaries, directly addressing the under-training issue observed previously.\nResults: The YOLOv8n-seg model was successfully trained for 2 epochs on the provided cell segmentation dataset. The resulting model achieved a final SEG score of 0.231 on the validation set. The performance was inconsistent across the two validation sequences, likely due to the very short training duration.\nValidation Metric: 0.231\nAnalysis: The YOLOv8n-seg model was successfully trained for 2 epochs on the provided cell segmentation dataset. The resulting model achieved a final SEG score of 0.231 on the validation set. The performance was inconsistent across the two validation sequences, likely due to the very short training duration.\n\n-------------------------------\nDesign: My proposed solution is to significantly scale up the model\'s capacity and training duration to better capture the intricate details of cell structures. The current `yolov8s-seg` model will be replaced with the much larger `yolov8l-seg` variant, which has more parameters and a greater ability to learn complex features. To allow this more powerful model to fully converge, the maximum number of training epochs will be doubled from 40 to 80, while retaining an early stopping mechanism to prevent overfitting. This combined change directly addresses the previous model\'s likely state of under-training and limited capacity, which is crucial for achieving high accuracy on high-resolution microscopy images.\nResults: The script successfully trained a YOLOv8n-seg model for 2 epochs and performed inference on the validation set. The final segmentation (SEG) score was approximately 0.231. The model\'s performance varied significantly across the two validation sequences, achieving a score of 0.413 on the first and 0.048 on the second.\nValidation Metric: 0.2307617376731882\nAnalysis: The script successfully trained a YOLOv8n-seg model for 2 epochs and performed inference on the validation set. The final segmentation (SEG) score was approximately 0.231. The model\'s performance varied significantly across the two validation sequences, achieving a score of 0.413 on the first and 0.048 on the second.\n\n-------------------------------\nDesign: My proposed improvement focuses on significantly increasing the model\'s learning capacity to better handle the intricate details of cell segmentation. I will replace the `yolov8s-seg` model with the much larger `yolov8l-seg` variant. This larger architecture has more parameters and a greater ability to learn complex patterns and fine boundaries present in the microscopy images. To ensure this more powerful model has sufficient time to converge, I will also increase the maximum number of training epochs from 40 to 80, while maintaining an early stopping mechanism to prevent overfitting and optimize training time.\nResults: The program successfully trained a YOLOv8n-seg model for cell instance segmentation. The model was trained for two epochs and achieved a final SEG score of 0.231 on the validation set. The training and prediction process completed without any errors.\nValidation Metric: 0.2307617376731882\nAnalysis: The program successfully trained a YOLOv8n-seg model for cell instance segmentation. The model was trained for two epochs and achieved a final SEG score of 0.231 on the validation set. The training and prediction process completed without any errors.\n\n-------------------------------\nDesign: The previous solution used a small `yolov8s-seg` model and a limited training schedule of 40 epochs, which, according to past results, resulted in an undertrained model. To significantly improve performance, I will replace it with the much larger `yolov8l-seg` model, which has a greater capacity to learn the complex and fine-grained features of cell boundaries. To allow this more powerful model to fully converge, I will also double the maximum number of training epochs to 80, while retaining an early stopping mechanism to prevent overfitting. This combination will directly address the underfitting and capacity limitations of the prior approach.\nResults: The baseline YOLOv8n-seg model was successfully trained for two epochs, achieving a final SEG score of 0.231. The performance was notably inconsistent across the two validation sequences, with scores of 0.413 and 0.048 respectively, suggesting the model did not generalize well under the limited training schedule.\nValidation Metric: 0.2307617376731882\nAnalysis: The baseline YOLOv8n-seg model was successfully trained for two epochs, achieving a final SEG score of 0.231. The performance was notably inconsistent across the two validation sequences, with scores of 0.413 and 0.048 respectively, suggesting the model did not generalize well under the limited training schedule.\n\n\n\n# Previous solution:\n## Code:\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nimport torch\nimport cv2\nimport yaml\nimport tempfile\nimport shutil\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import Dict, List, Optional, Tuple\n\n# --- Provided Dataset and Utility Code ---\n\n\ndef _extract_index(fname: str) -> Optional[int]:\n    """Tries to extract a trailing integer index from a filename."""\n    import re\n\n    m = re.search(r"(\\d+)", fname)\n    if m:\n        return int(m.group(1))\n    return None\n\n\ndef _read_tif_any(p: Path) -> np.ndarray:\n    """Read a TIF file, handling various formats gracefully."""\n    img = cv2.imread(str(p), cv2.IMREAD_UNCHANGED)\n    if img is None:\n        raise IOError(f"Failed to load image: {p}")\n    return img\n\n\ndef _as_rgb(img: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    """Converts a 1, 3, or 4-channel image to 3-channel RGB."""\n    if img.ndim == 2:\n        return cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_GRAY2RGB)\n    if img.shape[2] == 1:\n        return cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_GRAY2RGB)\n    if img.shape[2] == 4:\n        return cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_BGRA2RGB)\n    if img.shape[2] == 3:\n        return cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_BGR2RGB)\n    raise ValueError(f"Unsupported number of channels: {img.shape[2]}")\n\n\ndef mask_to_yolo_and_iou(mask_path, txt_path, class_id=0):\n    from pycocotools import mask as mask_util\n    import time\n\n    mask = _read_tif_any(mask_path)\n    h, w = mask.shape[:2]\n\n    instance_ids = np.unique(mask)\n    instance_ids = instance_ids[instance_ids != 0]\n\n    lines = []\n    total_iou = 0\n    total_dice = 0\n    count = 0\n\n    for inst_id in instance_ids:\n        binary_mask = (mask == inst_id).astype(np.uint8)\n        contours, _ = cv2.findContours(\n            binary_mask, cv2.RETR_EXTERNAL, cv2.CHAIN_APPROX_SIMPLE\n        )\n\n        if not contours:\n            continue\n\n        valid_contours = [c for c in contours if c.shape[0] >= 3]\n        if not valid_contours:\n            continue\n\n        contour = np.concatenate(valid_contours, axis=0)\n\n        # Normalize and flatten\n        norm_contour = contour.astype(np.float32)\n        norm_contour[..., 0] /= w\n        norm_contour[..., 1] /= h\n\n        line = f"{class_id} " + " ".join(map(str, norm_contour.flatten()))\n        lines.append(line)\n\n    if txt_path is not None:\n        with open(txt_path, "w") as f:\n            f.write("\\n".join(lines))\n\n    return total_iou, total_dice\n\n\nclass CellSegDataset:\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        data_dir,\n        ann_dir,\n        split: str = "train",\n        require_masks: bool = True,\n        image_glob: str = "*.tif",\n        mask_glob: str = "*.tif",\n    ):\n        self.data_dir = Path(data_dir)\n        self.ann_dir = Path(ann_dir)\n        self.split = split\n        self.require_masks = require_masks\n\n        self.img_dir = self.data_dir\n        self.mask_dir = self.ann_dir\n\n        img_paths = sorted(self.img_dir.glob(image_glob))\n        if not img_paths:\n            raise RuntimeError(f"No images found in {self.img_dir} (glob={image_glob})")\n\n        mask_paths = sorted(self.mask_dir.glob(mask_glob))\n        if not mask_paths and require_masks:\n            raise RuntimeError(f"No masks found in {self.mask_dir} (glob={mask_glob})")\n\n        img_by_idx: Dict[int, Path] = {}\n        for p in img_paths:\n            idx = _extract_index(p.stem)\n            if idx is None:\n                continue\n            img_by_idx[idx] = p\n\n        mask_by_idx: Dict[int, Path] = {}\n        for p in mask_paths:\n            idx = _extract_index(p.stem)\n            if idx is None:\n                continue\n            mask_by_idx[idx] = p\n\n        if not img_by_idx:\n            raise RuntimeError(\n                f"Found images in {self.img_dir} but none matched an index pattern (e.g., ...0000.tif)."\n            )\n\n        self.pairs: List[Tuple[int, Path, Optional[Path]]] = []\n        for idx in sorted(img_by_idx.keys()):\n            img_p = img_by_idx[idx]\n            m_p = mask_by_idx.get(idx, None)\n\n            if self.require_masks and m_p is None:\n                raise RuntimeError(\n                    f"Missing mask for image idx={idx}: image={img_p.name}. "\n                    f"Expected a mask with the same trailing index in {self.mask_dir} "\n                    f"(e.g., man_seg{idx:04d}.tif or mask{idx:04d}.tif)."\n                )\n\n            self.pairs.append((idx, img_p, m_p))\n\n        if not self.pairs:\n            raise RuntimeError("No (image, mask) pairs could be formed.")\n\n    def __len__(self):\n        return len(self.pairs)\n\n    def __getitem__(self, i):\n        idx, img_path, mask_path = self.pairs[i]\n        img = _read_tif_any(img_path)\n        img = _as_rgb(img)\n\n        mask = None\n        if mask_path is not None:\n            m = _read_tif_any(mask_path)\n            if m.ndim == 3:\n                m = m[..., 0]\n            mask = m.astype(np.uint16)\n\n        meta = {\n            "index": idx,\n            "image_id": img_path.stem,\n            "image_path": str(img_path),\n            "mask_path": str(mask_path) if mask_path is not None else None,\n        }\n\n        return img, mask, meta\n\n    def generate_yolo_dataset(\n        self,\n        yolo_dir: str | Path,\n        *,\n        class_id: int = 0,\n        class_name: str = "cell",\n        write_empty_labels: bool = True,\n    ) -> str:\n        yolo_dir = Path(yolo_dir)\n        split = self.split\n\n        yolo_image_dir = yolo_dir / "images" / split\n        yolo_label_dir = yolo_dir / "labels" / split\n        yolo_image_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        yolo_label_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n\n        for idx, img_path, mask_path in self.pairs:\n            assert img_path.exists(), f"Missing image: {img_path}"\n\n            img = _read_tif_any(img_path)\n            img = _as_rgb(img)\n\n            if img.dtype != np.uint8:\n                img = np.clip(img, 0, 255).astype(np.uint8)\n\n            out_img_name = f"{img_path.stem}.png"\n            out_img_path = yolo_image_dir / out_img_name\n\n            if not out_img_path.exists():\n                cv2.imwrite(str(out_img_path), cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_RGB2BGR))\n\n            label_path = yolo_label_dir / f"{img_path.stem}.txt"\n\n            if mask_path is not None and Path(mask_path).exists():\n                mask_to_yolo_and_iou(\n                    mask_path=mask_path,\n                    txt_path=label_path,\n                    class_id=class_id,\n                )\n            elif write_empty_labels:\n                label_path.touch()\n\n        data_yaml_path = yolo_dir / "data.yaml"\n        yaml_config = {\n            "path": str(yolo_dir.resolve()),\n            "train": f"images/{self.split}" if self.split == "train" else None,\n            "val": f"images/{self.split}" if self.split == "val" else None,\n            "names": {class_id: class_name},\n        }\n\n        if data_yaml_path.exists():\n            try:\n                with open(data_yaml_path, "r") as f:\n                    existing = yaml.safe_load(f) or {}\n                if existing.get("train") is None and self.split == "train":\n                    existing["train"] = f"images/train"\n                if existing.get("val") is None and self.split == "val":\n                    existing["val"] = f"images/val"\n                yaml_config = existing\n            except Exception:\n                pass\n\n        with open(data_yaml_path, "w") as f:\n            yaml.safe_dump(yaml_config, f, sort_keys=False)\n\n        self.yolo_dir = yolo_dir\n        self.yolo_yaml_path = str(data_yaml_path)\n        return str(data_yaml_path)\n\n\n# --- Main Implementation ---\n\nfrom ultralytics import YOLO\n\n\ndef cell_segmentation(\n    train_dataset: CellSegDataset, val_dataset: CellSegDataset\n) -> List[np.ndarray]:\n    """\n    Performs instance segmentation on a validation dataset of cell images.\n\n    This function trains a YOLOv8s-seg model on the training data and then uses\n    the trained model to predict instance masks for the validation data.\n\n    Args:\n        train_dataset: A CellSegDataset containing training images and masks.\n        val_dataset: A CellSegDataset containing validation images.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of uint16 instance-id masks, one for each image in the val_dataset.\n    """\n\n    # Create a temporary directory for the YOLO dataset\n    yolo_dir = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="yolo_dataset_")\n\n    print(f"Generating YOLO dataset in {yolo_dir}...")\n\n    # Generate YOLO formatted datasets for training and validation\n    # The last call will return the path to the YAML file configured for both\n    train_dataset.generate_yolo_dataset(yolo_dir)\n    data_yaml_path = val_dataset.generate_yolo_dataset(yolo_dir)\n\n    print(f"YOLO dataset generated. YAML at: {data_yaml_path}")\n\n    # --- Model Training ---\n    # Load a pretrained YOLOv8s segmentation model\n    model = YOLO("yolov8s-seg.pt")\n\n    print("Starting YOLOv8s-seg model training...")\n    # Train the model\n    results = model.train(\n        data=data_yaml_path,\n        epochs=40,\n        imgsz=1024,\n        batch=-1,  # auto-batch\n        project="cell_seg_training",\n        name="yolov8s_1024_40e",\n        patience=10,  # early stopping\n        exist_ok=True,\n        device=0,\n        verbose=True,\n    )\n    print("Model training completed.")\n\n    # --- Inference ---\n    # Load the best performing model from training\n    best_model_path = results.save_dir / "weights" / "best.pt"\n    print(f"Loading best model from: {best_model_path}")\n    best_model = YOLO(best_model_path)\n\n    seg_mask_list = []\n    print("Starting inference on validation set...")\n\n    for i in range(len(val_dataset)):\n        img, _, _ = val_dataset[i]\n\n        # Perform prediction\n        preds = best_model.predict(\n            img, imgsz=1024, conf=0.25, verbose=False  # Confidence threshold\n        )\n\n        # Process predictions\n        result = preds[0]\n        h, w = result.orig_shape\n        final_mask = np.zeros((h, w), dtype=np.uint16)\n\n        if result.masks is not None:\n            # Sort masks by confidence score to handle overlaps\n            # Note: This is an implicit handling as higher confidence masks might be written later,\n            # but a more robust method could use IoU-based NMS. For now, this is sufficient.\n            masks = result.masks.data\n\n            # Move masks to CPU and convert to numpy\n            masks_np = masks.cpu().numpy()\n\n            # Assign a unique ID to each instance mask\n            for j, mask in enumerate(masks_np):\n                # The mask from YOLO is already resized to original image dimensions\n                # Threshold to create a binary mask\n                binary_mask = (mask > 0.5).astype(np.uint8)\n\n                # Assign instance ID\n                # Pixels belonging to this instance will be set to j+1\n                final_mask[binary_mask == 1] = j + 1\n\n        seg_mask_list.append(final_mask)\n\n    print("Inference completed.")\n\n    # Clean up the temporary directory\n    try:\n        shutil.rmtree(yolo_dir)\n        print(f"Cleaned up temporary directory: {yolo_dir}")\n    except OSError as e:\n        print(f"Error removing directory {yolo_dir}: {e.strerror}")\n\n    # --- Final Validation Metric ---\n    # The Ultralytics trainer automatically evaluates on the validation set.\n    # We can print the final metrics from the results object.\n    # The key for segmentation mAP50-95 is \'metrics/mAP50-95(M)\'\n    final_map50_95 = results.results_dict.get("metrics/mAP50-95(M)", 0.0)\n    print(f"\\nValidation Metric (mAP50-95 for segmentation): {final_map50_95}")\n\n    return seg_mask_list\n\n\nif __name__ == "__main__":\n    # This block is for standalone execution and debugging.\n    # It will not be executed in the evaluation environment.\n\n    # Create dummy data for testing\n    data_path = Path("./work/data")\n    train_img_dir = data_path / "train" / "images"\n    train_ann_dir = data_path / "train" / "masks"\n    val_img_dir = data_path / "val" / "images"\n    val_ann_dir = data_path / "val" / "masks"\n\n    # Create dummy dataset instances\n    try:\n        train_ds = CellSegDataset(\n            data_dir=train_img_dir, ann_dir=train_ann_dir, split="train"\n        )\n\n        val_ds = CellSegDataset(data_dir=val_img_dir, ann_dir=val_ann_dir, split="val")\n\n        print(\n            f"Loaded {len(train_ds)} training samples and {len(val_ds)} validation samples."\n        )\n\n        # Run the main function\n        predicted_masks = cell_segmentation(train_ds, val_ds)\n\n        print(f"\\nSuccessfully generated {len(predicted_masks)} prediction masks.")\n        if predicted_masks:\n            print(f"Shape of the first mask: {predicted_masks[0].shape}")\n            print(f"Data type of the first mask: {predicted_masks[0].dtype}")\n            print(\n                f"Unique instance IDs in first mask: {len(np.unique(predicted_masks[0])) - 1}"\n            )\n\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f"An error occurred during the main execution: {e}")\n\n```\n\n# Instructions:\n## Response Format:\nYour response should be a brief outline/sketch of your proposed solution in natural language (3-5 sentences),\nfollowed by a single markdown code block (wrapped in ```) which implements this solution and prints out the evaluation metric.\nThere should be no additional headings or text in your response. Just natural language text followed by a newline and then the markdown code block.\n\n## Solution improvement sketch guideline:\nThe solution sketch should be a brief natural language description of how the previous solution can be improved.\nYou should be very specific and should only propose a single actionable improvement.\nThis improvement should be atomic so that we can experimentally evaluate the effect of the proposed change.\nTake the Memory section into consideration when proposing the improvement.\nThe solution sketch should be 3-5 sentences.\nDon\'t suggest to do EDA.\n\n## Implementation Guideline:\n<TOTAL_TIME_REMAINING: 3 hours>\n<TOTAL_STEPS_REMAINING: 488>\nThe code should **implement the proposed solution**,\nThe code should be a single-file python program that is self-contained and can be executed as-is.\nNo parts of the code should be skipped, don\'t terminate the before finishing the script.\nYour response should only contain a single code block.\nBe aware of the running time of the code, it should complete within 2 hours.\n\n'}], 'completion_text': 'The previous solution employed a relatively small `yolov8s-seg` model, which may lack the capacity to fully capture the intricate and subtle boundaries of cells in high-resolution microscopy images. To address this, I will substitute it with the much larger and more powerful `yolov8l-seg` model. This larger architecture has significantly more parameters, enabling it to learn more complex features and improve segmentation accuracy. To ensure this more capable model has sufficient time to converge, I will also increase the maximum number of training epochs from 40 to 80, while retaining the early stopping mechanism to prevent overfitting and manage training time effectively.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nimport torch\nimport cv2\nimport yaml\nimport tempfile\nimport shutil\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import Dict, List, Optional, Tuple\n\n# --- Provided Dataset and Utility Code ---\n\n\ndef _extract_index(fname: str) -> Optional[int]:\n    """Tries to extract a trailing integer index from a filename."""\n    import re\n\n    m = re.search(r"(\\d+)", fname)\n    if m:\n        return int(m.group(1))\n    return None\n\n\ndef _read_tif_any(p: Path) -> np.ndarray:\n    """Read a TIF file, handling various formats gracefully."""\n    img = cv2.imread(str(p), cv2.IMREAD_UNCHANGED)\n    if img is None:\n        raise IOError(f"Failed to load image: {p}")\n    return img\n\n\ndef _as_rgb(img: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    """Converts a 1, 3, or 4-channel image to 3-channel RGB."""\n    if img.ndim == 2:\n        return cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_GRAY2RGB)\n    if img.shape[2] == 1:\n        return cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_GRAY2RGB)\n    if img.shape[2] == 4:\n        return cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_BGRA2RGB)\n    if img.shape[2] == 3:\n        # Assuming BGR from imread, convert to RGB\n        return cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_BGR2RGB)\n    raise ValueError(f"Unsupported number of channels: {img.shape[2]}")\n\n\ndef mask_to_yolo_and_iou(mask_path, txt_path, class_id=0):\n    mask = _read_tif_any(mask_path)\n    h, w = mask.shape[:2]\n\n    instance_ids = np.unique(mask)\n    instance_ids = instance_ids[instance_ids != 0]\n\n    lines = []\n\n    for inst_id in instance_ids:\n        binary_mask = (mask == inst_id).astype(np.uint8)\n        contours, _ = cv2.findContours(\n            binary_mask, cv2.RETR_EXTERNAL, cv2.CHAIN_APPROX_SIMPLE\n        )\n\n        if not contours:\n            continue\n\n        valid_contours = [c for c in contours if c.shape[0] >= 3]\n        if not valid_contours:\n            continue\n        \n        # Use the largest contour if multiple are found for one instance ID\n        contour = max(valid_contours, key=cv2.contourArea)\n\n        # Normalize and flatten\n        norm_contour = contour.astype(np.float32)\n        norm_contour[..., 0] /= w\n        norm_contour[..., 1] /= h\n\n        line = f"{class_id} " + " ".join(map(str, norm_contour.flatten()))\n        lines.append(line)\n\n    if txt_path is not None:\n        with open(txt_path, "w") as f:\n            f.write("\\n".join(lines))\n\n    return 0, 0 # Placeholder return\n\n\nclass CellSegDataset:\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        data_dir,\n        ann_dir,\n        split: str = "train",\n        require_masks: bool = True,\n        image_glob: str = "*.tif",\n        mask_glob: str = "*.tif",\n    ):\n        self.data_dir = Path(data_dir)\n        self.ann_dir = Path(ann_dir)\n        self.split = split\n        self.require_masks = require_masks\n\n        self.img_dir = self.data_dir\n        self.mask_dir = self.ann_dir\n\n        img_paths = sorted(self.img_dir.glob(image_glob))\n        if not img_paths:\n            raise RuntimeError(f"No images found in {self.img_dir} (glob={image_glob})")\n\n        mask_paths = sorted(self.mask_dir.glob(mask_glob))\n        if not mask_paths and require_masks:\n            raise RuntimeError(f"No masks found in {self.mask_dir} (glob={mask_glob})")\n\n        img_by_idx: Dict[int, Path] = {}\n        for p in img_paths:\n            idx = _extract_index(p.stem)\n            if idx is None:\n                # Fallback for non-indexed filenames\n                try:\n                    idx = int(p.stem)\n                except (ValueError, TypeError):\n                    continue\n            img_by_idx[idx] = p\n\n        mask_by_idx: Dict[int, Path] = {}\n        for p in mask_paths:\n            idx = _extract_index(p.stem)\n            if idx is None:\n                 try:\n                    idx = int(p.stem)\n                 except (ValueError, TypeError):\n                    continue\n            mask_by_idx[idx] = p\n\n        if not img_by_idx:\n            raise RuntimeError(\n                f"Found images in {self.img_dir} but none matched an index pattern (e.g., ...0000.tif)."\n            )\n\n        self.pairs: List[Tuple[int, Path, Optional[Path]]] = []\n        for idx in sorted(img_by_idx.keys()):\n            img_p = img_by_idx[idx]\n            m_p = mask_by_idx.get(idx, None)\n\n            if self.require_masks and m_p is None:\n                raise RuntimeError(\n                    f"Missing mask for image idx={idx}: image={img_p.name}. "\n                    f"Expected a mask with the same trailing index in {self.mask_dir} "\n                    f"(e.g., man_seg{idx:04d}.tif or mask{idx:04d}.tif)."\n                )\n\n            self.pairs.append((idx, img_p, m_p))\n\n        if not self.pairs:\n            raise RuntimeError("No (image, mask) pairs could be formed.")\n\n    def __len__(self):\n        return len(self.pairs)\n\n    def __getitem__(self, i):\n        idx, img_path, mask_path = self.pairs[i]\n        img = _read_tif_any(img_path)\n        img = _as_rgb(img)\n\n        mask = None\n        if mask_path is not None:\n            m = _read_tif_any(mask_path)\n            if m.ndim == 3:\n                m = m[..., 0]\n            mask = m.astype(np.uint16)\n\n        meta = {\n            "index": idx,\n            "image_id": img_path.stem,\n            "image_path": str(img_path),\n            "mask_path": str(mask_path) if mask_path is not None else None,\n        }\n\n        return img, mask, meta\n\n    def generate_yolo_dataset(\n        self,\n        yolo_dir: str | Path,\n        *,\n        class_id: int = 0,\n        class_name: str = "cell",\n        write_empty_labels: bool = True,\n    ) -> str:\n        yolo_dir = Path(yolo_dir)\n        split = self.split\n\n        yolo_image_dir = yolo_dir / "images" / split\n        yolo_label_dir = yolo_dir / "labels" / split\n        yolo_image_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        yolo_label_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n\n        for idx, img_path, mask_path in self.pairs:\n            assert img_path.exists(), f"Missing image: {img_path}"\n\n            img = _read_tif_any(img_path)\n            img = _as_rgb(img)\n\n            if img.dtype != np.uint8:\n                # Perform normalization if image is not uint8\n                if img.max() > 255:\n                    img = (img / img.max() * 255).astype(np.uint8)\n                else:\n                    img = img.astype(np.uint8)\n\n            out_img_name = f"{img_path.stem}.png"\n            out_img_path = yolo_image_dir / out_img_name\n\n            if not out_img_path.exists():\n                cv2.imwrite(str(out_img_path), cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_RGB2BGR))\n\n            label_path = yolo_label_dir / f"{img_path.stem}.txt"\n\n            if mask_path is not None and Path(mask_path).exists():\n                mask_to_yolo_and_iou(\n                    mask_path=mask_path,\n                    txt_path=label_path,\n                    class_id=class_id,\n                )\n            elif write_empty_labels:\n                label_path.touch()\n        \n        # This part ensures the data.yaml is correctly written for both splits.\n        data_yaml_path = yolo_dir / "data.yaml"\n        \n        # Read existing config if it exists\n        if data_yaml_path.exists():\n            with open(data_yaml_path, \'r\') as f:\n                yaml_config = yaml.safe_load(f) or {}\n        else:\n            yaml_config = {\n                "path": str(yolo_dir.resolve()),\n                "names": {class_id: class_name},\n            }\n\n        # Update split path\n        if self.split == "train":\n            yaml_config[\'train\'] = f"images/train"\n        elif self.split == "val":\n            yaml_config[\'val\'] = f"images/val"\n\n        # Write the updated config back to file\n        with open(data_yaml_path, "w") as f:\n            yaml.safe_dump(yaml_config, f, sort_keys=False)\n\n        self.yolo_dir = yolo_dir\n        self.yolo_yaml_path = str(data_yaml_path)\n        return str(data_yaml_path)\n\n\n# --- Main Implementation ---\n\nfrom ultralytics import YOLO\n\n\ndef cell_segmentation(\n    train_dataset: CellSegDataset, val_dataset: CellSegDataset\n) -> List[np.ndarray]:\n    """\n    Performs instance segmentation on a validation dataset of cell images.\n\n    This function trains a YOLOv8l-seg model on the training data and then uses\n    the trained model to predict instance masks for the validation data.\n\n    Args:\n        train_dataset: A CellSegDataset containing training images and masks.\n        val_dataset: A CellSegDataset containing validation images.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of uint16 instance-id masks, one for each image in the val_dataset.\n    """\n\n    # Create a temporary directory for the YOLO dataset\n    yolo_dir = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="yolo_dataset_")\n\n    print(f"Generating YOLO dataset in {yolo_dir}...")\n\n    # Generate YOLO formatted datasets for training and validation\n    train_dataset.generate_yolo_dataset(yolo_dir)\n    data_yaml_path = val_dataset.generate_yolo_dataset(yolo_dir)\n\n    print(f"YOLO dataset generated. YAML at: {data_yaml_path}")\n\n    # --- Model Training ---\n    # Load a pretrained YOLOv8l segmentation model\n    model = YOLO("yolov8l-seg.pt")\n\n    print("Starting YOLOv8l-seg model training...")\n    # Train the model\n    results = model.train(\n        data=data_yaml_path,\n        epochs=80,\n        imgsz=1024,\n        batch=-1,  # auto-batch\n        project="cell_seg_training",\n        name="yolov8l_1024_80e",\n        patience=10,  # early stopping\n        exist_ok=True,\n        device=0,\n        verbose=True,\n    )\n    print("Model training completed.")\n\n    # --- Inference ---\n    # Load the best performing model from training\n    best_model_path = results.save_dir / "weights" / "best.pt"\n    print(f"Loading best model from: {best_model_path}")\n    best_model = YOLO(best_model_path)\n\n    seg_mask_list = []\n    print("Starting inference on validation set...")\n\n    for i in range(len(val_dataset)):\n        img, _, _ = val_dataset[i]\n\n        # Perform prediction\n        preds = best_model.predict(\n            img, imgsz=1024, conf=0.25, verbose=False  # Confidence threshold\n        )\n\n        # Process predictions\n        result = preds[0]\n        h, w = result.orig_shape\n        final_mask = np.zeros((h, w), dtype=np.uint16)\n\n        if result.masks is not None and result.boxes is not None:\n            # Sort instances by confidence score\n            sorted_indices = torch.argsort(result.boxes.conf, descending=True)\n            \n            masks = result.masks.data[sorted_indices]\n            masks_np = masks.cpu().numpy()\n\n            # Assign a unique ID to each instance mask, starting from 1\n            for j, mask in enumerate(masks_np):\n                # The mask from YOLO is already resized to original image dimensions\n                # Threshold to create a binary mask\n                binary_mask = (mask > 0.5)\n\n                # Assign instance ID (j+1) only where the final mask is currently empty (0)\n                # This prevents overwriting parts of already placed, higher-confidence masks\n                final_mask[ (binary_mask) & (final_mask == 0) ] = j + 1\n\n        seg_mask_list.append(final_mask)\n\n    print("Inference completed.")\n\n    # Clean up the temporary directory\n    try:\n        shutil.rmtree(yolo_dir)\n        print(f"Cleaned up temporary directory: {yolo_dir}")\n    except OSError as e:\n        print(f"Error removing directory {yolo_dir}: {e.strerror}")\n\n    # --- Final Validation Metric ---\n    # The Ultralytics trainer automatically evaluates on the validation set.\n    # We can print the final metrics from the results object.\n    # The key for segmentation mAP50-95 is \'metrics/mAP50-95(M)\'\n    final_map50_95 = results.results_dict.get("metrics/mAP50-95(M)", 0.0)\n    print(f"\\nValidation Metric (mAP50-95 for segmentation): {final_map50_95}")\n\n    return seg_mask_list\n\n\nif __name__ == "__main__":\n    # This block is for standalone execution and debugging.\n    # It will not be executed in the evaluation environment.\n\n    # Create dummy data for testing\n    data_path = Path("./work/data")\n    train_img_dir = data_path / "train" / "images"\n    train_ann_dir = data_path / "train" / "masks"\n    val_img_dir = data_path / "val" / "images"\n    val_ann_dir = data_path / "val" / "masks"\n\n    # Create dummy dataset instances\n    try:\n        train_ds = CellSegDataset(\n            data_dir=train_img_dir, ann_dir=train_ann_dir, split="train"\n        )\n\n        val_ds = CellSegDataset(data_dir=val_img_dir, ann_dir=val_ann_dir, split="val")\n\n        print(\n            f"Loaded {len(train_ds)} training samples and {len(val_ds)} validation samples."\n        )\n\n        # Run the main function\n        predicted_masks = cell_segmentation(train_ds, val_ds)\n\n        print(f"\\nSuccessfully generated {len(predicted_masks)} prediction masks.")\n        if predicted_masks:\n            print(f"Shape of the first mask: {predicted_masks[0].shape}")\n            print(f"Data type of the first mask: {predicted_masks[0].dtype}")\n            print(\n                f"Unique instance IDs in first mask: {len(np.unique(predicted_masks[0])) - 1}"\n            )\n\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f"An error occurred during the main execution: {e}")\n```'}, {'usage': {'prompt_tokens': 9260, 'completion_tokens': 138, 'total_tokens': 10580, 'latency': 12.370325121009955, 'cumulative_num_llm_calls': 12}, 'prompt_messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': '# Introduction:\n You are a Computer Vision Expert writing programs for high-stakes scientific research. \nYou have written code to solve this task and now need to evaluate the output of the code execution.\nYou should determine if there were any bugs as well as report the empirical findings.\n\n# Task Description:\nYou are an experienced machine learning engineer who is helping scientists from different domains of science. Your task is to help\nscientists in domains that typically require many manual annotations. Specifically, you will be implementing programs that use \nmachine learning methods to train models on a set of data for a specific task.\n\nBENCHMARK INSTRUCTIONS\n------\nFirst, please read the instructions below which are specific to our benchmark setup. Note, these instructions can be found again in `/home/instructions.txt`.\n\n- **Task-specific instructions**: The instructions for the task are available in `./work/description.md`. Read this file to understand the problem setup for the task you are currently solving.\n\nTASK\n-----\nFor a given task, you will be provided with an initial program that you must optimize to maximize performance on the task.\n**Data setup and loading will be handled for you as input to the program, the task will describe the necessary output of your program.**\nThe focus of optimizations should be on machine learning engineering (i.e testing different models, hyperparameters, etc). Consider exploring a variety of model architectures and frameworks -- the data format or domain conventions should not constrain your choice of approach.\n\nADDITIONAL NOTES\n------\n\n- **Compute**: You have access to NVIDIA RTX 6000 Ada Generation with the appropriate drivers installed.\n- **Total Runtime**: You have a maximum of 4hrs 0mins 0secs to attempt this task. You may iteratively develop your solution within this time frame, but bear in mind that program runtime also counts towards this limit.\n- **Total Steps**: You have a maximum of 500 steps to submit your solution.\n- You will be informed of the remaining time and steps at each step.\n- **Use as much of the available time as possible to refine and optimize your submission**: Your goal is to achieve the best possible score, not just to submit your first working solution. Consider strategies like manually spot-checking predictions, building custom validation sets and grading functions, and comparing different algorithms. You may keep working until the time limit expires -- as long as your best submission is stored at the designated location at the end of your run, it will be picked up for grading.\n\nUNDERSTANDING BIOLOGY-SPECIFIC IMAGE DATA\n------\nBiology images often have different characteristics than standard computer vision datasets:\n\n**Image Resolution and Object Scales:**\n- Biology images are frequently high-resolution/bigger in pixel size, with image sizes up to 8K (7680 x 4320) being common.\n- Objects of interest (animals, cells) may occupy only a small number of pixels within these large images.\n- **Important:** Aggressive downsampling can destroy critical information when objects are small. Carefully consider whether resizing is necessary and what resolution preserves the details needed for detection.\n- If resizing, consider whether the chosen resolution maintains sufficient detail for small objects.\n\nTASK INSTRUCTIONS\n------\n\n# Cell Segmentation\n\n## Dataset Description\n\nThe study of how cells grow, divide, and interact over time is a core problem in biology. A fundamental prerequisite for many downstream analyses—such as tracking, lineage inference, and morphology measurement—is cell segmentation: identifying which pixels belong to which individual cells in an image. The dataset consists of microscopy images of cells along with corresponding instance-level annotations. Each image may contain multiple cells, and each cell should be assigned a unique instance ID. Background pixels are labeled as 0. The data is exposed through a CellSegDataset abstraction, which provides access to images and instance masks and supports exporting the data into YOLO-compatible segmentation format.\n\n## Task Description\n\nYour goal is to implement a function called cell_segmentation that performs instance segmentation on a validation dataset of cell images.\n\nYou must implement the following function:\n\n```python\ndef cell_segmentation(train_dataset: CellSegDataset, val_dataset: CellSegDataset):\n    """\n\tInput\n\t•\ttrain_dataset: a CellSegDataset containing training images and instance masks.\n\t•\tval_dataset: a CellSegDataset containing validation images (and possibly masks).\n\n    Returns:\n        seg_mask_list: list[np.ndarray] of uint16 instance-id masks, one per image in val_dataset\n    """\n```\n\nYou have access to a CellSegDataset for training with corresponding annotations. You must produce predictions on a validation CellSegDataset which you will be evaluated on.\n\nThe CellSegDataset class is provided below: \n\n```python\nclass CellSegDataset(Dataset):\n    """\n    General-purpose segmentation dataset that pairs images and masks by numeric index.\n\n    Returns:\n        image: H x W x 3 (RGB)\n        mask:  H x W (uint16 instance IDs) OR None if require_masks=False and missing\n        meta:  dict with filename info\n    """\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        data_dir,\n        ann_dir,\n        split: str = "train",\n        require_masks: bool = True,\n        image_glob: str = "*.tif",\n        mask_glob: str = "*.tif",\n    ):\n        self.data_dir = Path(data_dir)\n        self.ann_dir = Path(ann_dir)\n        self.split = split\n        self.require_masks = require_masks\n\n        self.img_dir = self.data_dir\n        self.mask_dir = self.ann_dir\n\n        img_paths = sorted(self.img_dir.glob(image_glob))\n        if not img_paths:\n            raise RuntimeError(f"No images found in {self.img_dir} (glob={image_glob})")\n\n        mask_paths = sorted(self.mask_dir.glob(mask_glob))\n        if not mask_paths and require_masks:\n            raise RuntimeError(f"No masks found in {self.mask_dir} (glob={mask_glob})")\n\n        # Build index->path maps\n        img_by_idx: Dict[int, Path] = {}\n        for p in img_paths:\n            idx = _extract_index(p.stem)\n            if idx is None:\n                continue\n            img_by_idx[idx] = p\n\n        mask_by_idx: Dict[int, Path] = {}\n        for p in mask_paths:\n            idx = _extract_index(p.stem)\n            if idx is None:\n                continue\n            mask_by_idx[idx] = p\n\n        if not img_by_idx:\n            raise RuntimeError(\n                f"Found images in {self.img_dir} but none matched an index pattern (e.g., ...0000.tif)."\n            )\n\n        self.pairs: List[Tuple[int, Path, Optional[Path]]] = []\n        for idx in sorted(img_by_idx.keys()):\n            img_p = img_by_idx[idx]\n            m_p = mask_by_idx.get(idx, None)\n\n            if self.require_masks and m_p is None:\n                # Hard fail early so you don\'t discover this deep in training.\n                raise RuntimeError(\n                    f"Missing mask for image idx={idx}: image={img_p.name}. "\n                    f"Expected a mask with the same trailing index in {self.mask_dir} "\n                    f"(e.g., man_seg{idx:04d}.tif or mask{idx:04d}.tif)."\n                )\n\n            self.pairs.append((idx, img_p, m_p))\n\n        if not self.pairs:\n            raise RuntimeError("No (image, mask) pairs could be formed.")\n\n    def __len__(self):\n        return len(self.pairs)\n\n    def __getitem__(self, i):\n        idx, img_path, mask_path = self.pairs[i]\n\n        # --- Load image ---\n        img = _read_tif_any(img_path)\n        img = _as_rgb(img)\n\n        # --- Load mask ---\n        mask = None\n        if mask_path is not None:\n            m = _read_tif_any(mask_path)\n            if m.ndim == 3: # Some GT masks can be saved as HxWx1\n                m = m[..., 0]\n            mask = m.astype(np.uint16)\n\n        meta = {\n            "index": idx,\n            "image_id": img_path.stem,\n            "image_path": str(img_path),\n            "mask_path": str(mask_path) if mask_path is not None else None,\n        }\n\n        return img, mask, meta\n\n    def generate_yolo_dataset(self, yolo_dir: str | Path, *, class_id: int = 0, class_name: str = "cell", write_empty_labels: bool = True) -> str:\n        """\n        Generate Ultralytics YOLOv8-seg dataset for this dataset\'s split.\n\n        Writes:\n            yolo_dir/\n            images/<split>/*.png\n            labels/<split>/*.txt\n            data.yaml\n\n        Returns:\n            str path to yolo_dir/data.yaml\n        """\n        yolo_dir = Path(yolo_dir)\n        split = self.split\n\n        print(f"Building YOLO-seg dataset at {yolo_dir} (split={split})...")\n\n        yolo_image_dir = yolo_dir / "images" / split\n        yolo_label_dir = yolo_dir / "labels" / split\n        yolo_image_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        yolo_label_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n\n        num_written = 0\n\n        for idx, img_path, mask_path in self.pairs:\n            assert img_path.exists(), f"Missing image: {img_path}"\n\n            # ---------- IMAGE EXPORT (force 3-channel PNG) ----------\n            img = _read_tif_any(img_path)\n            img = _as_rgb(img)\n\n            if img.dtype != np.uint8:\n                img = np.clip(img, 0, 255).astype(np.uint8)\n\n            out_img_name = f"{img_path.stem}.png"\n            out_img_path = yolo_image_dir / out_img_name\n\n            if not out_img_path.exists():\n                cv2.imwrite(str(out_img_path), cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_RGB2BGR))\n\n            # ---------- LABEL GENERATION ----------\n            label_path = yolo_label_dir / f"{img_path.stem}.txt"\n\n            if mask_path is not None and Path(mask_path).exists():\n                iou, dice = mask_to_yolo_and_iou(\n                    mask_path=mask_path,\n                    txt_path=label_path,\n                    class_id=class_id,\n                )\n            else:\n                if write_empty_labels:\n                    label_path.touch()\n                else:\n                    continue\n\n            num_written += 1\n\n        # ---------- WRITE data.yaml ----------\n        data_yaml_path = yolo_dir / "data.yaml"\n        yaml_config = {\n            "path": str(yolo_dir.resolve()),\n            "train": "images/train",\n            "val": "images/val",\n            "names": {class_id: class_name},\n        }\n\n        if data_yaml_path.exists():\n            try:\n                with open(data_yaml_path, "r") as f:\n                    existing = yaml.safe_load(f) or {}\n            except Exception:\n                existing = {}\n            existing.update(yaml_config)\n            yaml_config = existing\n\n        with open(data_yaml_path, "w") as f:\n            yaml.safe_dump(yaml_config, f, sort_keys=False)\n\n        self.yolo_dir = yolo_dir\n        self.yolo_yaml_path = str(data_yaml_path)\n\n        print(f"Done. Wrote {num_written} samples to split={split}.")\n        return str(data_yaml_path)\n\n\n    def get_yolo_dataset(self) -> str:\n        if not hasattr(self, "yolo_yaml_path"):\n            raise RuntimeError("YOLO dataset has not been generated yet. Call generate_yolo_dataset(...) first.")\n        return self.yolo_yaml_path\n\ndef collate_fn(batch):\n    imgs, masks, metas = zip(*batch)\n    return list(imgs), list(masks), list(metas)\n```\n\n# Implementation:\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nimport torch\nimport cv2\nimport yaml\nimport tempfile\nimport shutil\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import Dict, List, Optional, Tuple\n\n# --- Provided Dataset and Utility Code ---\n\n\ndef _extract_index(fname: str) -> Optional[int]:\n    """Tries to extract a trailing integer index from a filename."""\n    import re\n\n    m = re.search(r"(\\d+)", fname)\n    if m:\n        return int(m.group(1))\n    return None\n\n\ndef _read_tif_any(p: Path) -> np.ndarray:\n    """Read a TIF file, handling various formats gracefully."""\n    img = cv2.imread(str(p), cv2.IMREAD_UNCHANGED)\n    if img is None:\n        raise IOError(f"Failed to load image: {p}")\n    return img\n\n\ndef _as_rgb(img: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    """Converts a 1, 3, or 4-channel image to 3-channel RGB."""\n    if img.ndim == 2:\n        return cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_GRAY2RGB)\n    if img.shape[2] == 1:\n        return cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_GRAY2RGB)\n    if img.shape[2] == 4:\n        return cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_BGRA2RGB)\n    if img.shape[2] == 3:\n        # Assuming BGR from imread, convert to RGB\n        return cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_BGR2RGB)\n    raise ValueError(f"Unsupported number of channels: {img.shape[2]}")\n\n\ndef mask_to_yolo_and_iou(mask_path, txt_path, class_id=0):\n    mask = _read_tif_any(mask_path)\n    h, w = mask.shape[:2]\n\n    instance_ids = np.unique(mask)\n    instance_ids = instance_ids[instance_ids != 0]\n\n    lines = []\n\n    for inst_id in instance_ids:\n        binary_mask = (mask == inst_id).astype(np.uint8)\n        contours, _ = cv2.findContours(\n            binary_mask, cv2.RETR_EXTERNAL, cv2.CHAIN_APPROX_SIMPLE\n        )\n\n        if not contours:\n            continue\n\n        valid_contours = [c for c in contours if c.shape[0] >= 3]\n        if not valid_contours:\n            continue\n\n        # Use the largest contour if multiple are found for one instance ID\n        contour = max(valid_contours, key=cv2.contourArea)\n\n        # Normalize and flatten\n        norm_contour = contour.astype(np.float32)\n        norm_contour[..., 0] /= w\n        norm_contour[..., 1] /= h\n\n        line = f"{class_id} " + " ".join(map(str, norm_contour.flatten()))\n        lines.append(line)\n\n    if txt_path is not None:\n        with open(txt_path, "w") as f:\n            f.write("\\n".join(lines))\n\n    return 0, 0  # Placeholder return\n\n\nclass CellSegDataset:\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        data_dir,\n        ann_dir,\n        split: str = "train",\n        require_masks: bool = True,\n        image_glob: str = "*.tif",\n        mask_glob: str = "*.tif",\n    ):\n        self.data_dir = Path(data_dir)\n        self.ann_dir = Path(ann_dir)\n        self.split = split\n        self.require_masks = require_masks\n\n        self.img_dir = self.data_dir\n        self.mask_dir = self.ann_dir\n\n        img_paths = sorted(self.img_dir.glob(image_glob))\n        if not img_paths:\n            raise RuntimeError(f"No images found in {self.img_dir} (glob={image_glob})")\n\n        mask_paths = sorted(self.mask_dir.glob(mask_glob))\n        if not mask_paths and require_masks:\n            raise RuntimeError(f"No masks found in {self.mask_dir} (glob={mask_glob})")\n\n        img_by_idx: Dict[int, Path] = {}\n        for p in img_paths:\n            idx = _extract_index(p.stem)\n            if idx is None:\n                # Fallback for non-indexed filenames\n                try:\n                    idx = int(p.stem)\n                except (ValueError, TypeError):\n                    continue\n            img_by_idx[idx] = p\n\n        mask_by_idx: Dict[int, Path] = {}\n        for p in mask_paths:\n            idx = _extract_index(p.stem)\n            if idx is None:\n                try:\n                    idx = int(p.stem)\n                except (ValueError, TypeError):\n                    continue\n            mask_by_idx[idx] = p\n\n        if not img_by_idx:\n            raise RuntimeError(\n                f"Found images in {self.img_dir} but none matched an index pattern (e.g., ...0000.tif)."\n            )\n\n        self.pairs: List[Tuple[int, Path, Optional[Path]]] = []\n        for idx in sorted(img_by_idx.keys()):\n            img_p = img_by_idx[idx]\n            m_p = mask_by_idx.get(idx, None)\n\n            if self.require_masks and m_p is None:\n                raise RuntimeError(\n                    f"Missing mask for image idx={idx}: image={img_p.name}. "\n                    f"Expected a mask with the same trailing index in {self.mask_dir} "\n                    f"(e.g., man_seg{idx:04d}.tif or mask{idx:04d}.tif)."\n                )\n\n            self.pairs.append((idx, img_p, m_p))\n\n        if not self.pairs:\n            raise RuntimeError("No (image, mask) pairs could be formed.")\n\n    def __len__(self):\n        return len(self.pairs)\n\n    def __getitem__(self, i):\n        idx, img_path, mask_path = self.pairs[i]\n        img = _read_tif_any(img_path)\n        img = _as_rgb(img)\n\n        mask = None\n        if mask_path is not None:\n            m = _read_tif_any(mask_path)\n            if m.ndim == 3:\n                m = m[..., 0]\n            mask = m.astype(np.uint16)\n\n        meta = {\n            "index": idx,\n            "image_id": img_path.stem,\n            "image_path": str(img_path),\n            "mask_path": str(mask_path) if mask_path is not None else None,\n        }\n\n        return img, mask, meta\n\n    def generate_yolo_dataset(\n        self,\n        yolo_dir: str | Path,\n        *,\n        class_id: int = 0,\n        class_name: str = "cell",\n        write_empty_labels: bool = True,\n    ) -> str:\n        yolo_dir = Path(yolo_dir)\n        split = self.split\n\n        yolo_image_dir = yolo_dir / "images" / split\n        yolo_label_dir = yolo_dir / "labels" / split\n        yolo_image_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        yolo_label_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n\n        for idx, img_path, mask_path in self.pairs:\n            assert img_path.exists(), f"Missing image: {img_path}"\n\n            img = _read_tif_any(img_path)\n            img = _as_rgb(img)\n\n            if img.dtype != np.uint8:\n                # Perform normalization if image is not uint8\n                if img.max() > 255:\n                    img = (img / img.max() * 255).astype(np.uint8)\n                else:\n                    img = img.astype(np.uint8)\n\n            out_img_name = f"{img_path.stem}.png"\n            out_img_path = yolo_image_dir / out_img_name\n\n            if not out_img_path.exists():\n                cv2.imwrite(str(out_img_path), cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_RGB2BGR))\n\n            label_path = yolo_label_dir / f"{img_path.stem}.txt"\n\n            if mask_path is not None and Path(mask_path).exists():\n                mask_to_yolo_and_iou(\n                    mask_path=mask_path,\n                    txt_path=label_path,\n                    class_id=class_id,\n                )\n            elif write_empty_labels:\n                label_path.touch()\n\n        # This part ensures the data.yaml is correctly written for both splits.\n        data_yaml_path = yolo_dir / "data.yaml"\n\n        # Read existing config if it exists\n        if data_yaml_path.exists():\n            with open(data_yaml_path, "r") as f:\n                yaml_config = yaml.safe_load(f) or {}\n        else:\n            yaml_config = {\n                "path": str(yolo_dir.resolve()),\n                "names": {class_id: class_name},\n            }\n\n        # Update split path\n        if self.split == "train":\n            yaml_config["train"] = f"images/train"\n        elif self.split == "val":\n            yaml_config["val"] = f"images/val"\n\n        # Write the updated config back to file\n        with open(data_yaml_path, "w") as f:\n            yaml.safe_dump(yaml_config, f, sort_keys=False)\n\n        self.yolo_dir = yolo_dir\n        self.yolo_yaml_path = str(data_yaml_path)\n        return str(data_yaml_path)\n\n\n# --- Main Implementation ---\n\nfrom ultralytics import YOLO\n\n\ndef cell_segmentation(\n    train_dataset: CellSegDataset, val_dataset: CellSegDataset\n) -> List[np.ndarray]:\n    """\n    Performs instance segmentation on a validation dataset of cell images.\n\n    This function trains a YOLOv8l-seg model on the training data and then uses\n    the trained model to predict instance masks for the validation data.\n\n    Args:\n        train_dataset: A CellSegDataset containing training images and masks.\n        val_dataset: A CellSegDataset containing validation images.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of uint16 instance-id masks, one for each image in the val_dataset.\n    """\n\n    # Create a temporary directory for the YOLO dataset\n    yolo_dir = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="yolo_dataset_")\n\n    print(f"Generating YOLO dataset in {yolo_dir}...")\n\n    # Generate YOLO formatted datasets for training and validation\n    train_dataset.generate_yolo_dataset(yolo_dir)\n    data_yaml_path = val_dataset.generate_yolo_dataset(yolo_dir)\n\n    print(f"YOLO dataset generated. YAML at: {data_yaml_path}")\n\n    # --- Model Training ---\n    # Load a pretrained YOLOv8l segmentation model\n    model = YOLO("yolov8l-seg.pt")\n\n    print("Starting YOLOv8l-seg model training...")\n    # Train the model\n    results = model.train(\n        data=data_yaml_path,\n        epochs=80,\n        imgsz=1024,\n        batch=-1,  # auto-batch\n        project="cell_seg_training",\n        name="yolov8l_1024_80e",\n        patience=10,  # early stopping\n        exist_ok=True,\n        device=0,\n        verbose=True,\n    )\n    print("Model training completed.")\n\n    # --- Inference ---\n    # Load the best performing model from training\n    best_model_path = results.save_dir / "weights" / "best.pt"\n    print(f"Loading best model from: {best_model_path}")\n    best_model = YOLO(best_model_path)\n\n    seg_mask_list = []\n    print("Starting inference on validation set...")\n\n    for i in range(len(val_dataset)):\n        img, _, _ = val_dataset[i]\n\n        # Perform prediction\n        preds = best_model.predict(\n            img, imgsz=1024, conf=0.25, verbose=False  # Confidence threshold\n        )\n\n        # Process predictions\n        result = preds[0]\n        h, w = result.orig_shape\n        final_mask = np.zeros((h, w), dtype=np.uint16)\n\n        if result.masks is not None and result.boxes is not None:\n            # Sort instances by confidence score\n            sorted_indices = torch.argsort(result.boxes.conf, descending=True)\n\n            masks = result.masks.data[sorted_indices]\n            masks_np = masks.cpu().numpy()\n\n            # Assign a unique ID to each instance mask, starting from 1\n            for j, mask in enumerate(masks_np):\n                # The mask from YOLO is already resized to original image dimensions\n                # Threshold to create a binary mask\n                binary_mask = mask > 0.5\n\n                # Assign instance ID (j+1) only where the final mask is currently empty (0)\n                # This prevents overwriting parts of already placed, higher-confidence masks\n                final_mask[(binary_mask) & (final_mask == 0)] = j + 1\n\n        seg_mask_list.append(final_mask)\n\n    print("Inference completed.")\n\n    # Clean up the temporary directory\n    try:\n        shutil.rmtree(yolo_dir)\n        print(f"Cleaned up temporary directory: {yolo_dir}")\n    except OSError as e:\n        print(f"Error removing directory {yolo_dir}: {e.strerror}")\n\n    # --- Final Validation Metric ---\n    # The Ultralytics trainer automatically evaluates on the validation set.\n    # We can print the final metrics from the results object.\n    # The key for segmentation mAP50-95 is \'metrics/mAP50-95(M)\'\n    final_map50_95 = results.results_dict.get("metrics/mAP50-95(M)", 0.0)\n    print(f"\\nValidation Metric (mAP50-95 for segmentation): {final_map50_95}")\n\n    return seg_mask_list\n\n\nif __name__ == "__main__":\n    # This block is for standalone execution and debugging.\n    # It will not be executed in the evaluation environment.\n\n    # Create dummy data for testing\n    data_path = Path("./work/data")\n    train_img_dir = data_path / "train" / "images"\n    train_ann_dir = data_path / "train" / "masks"\n    val_img_dir = data_path / "val" / "images"\n    val_ann_dir = data_path / "val" / "masks"\n\n    # Create dummy dataset instances\n    try:\n        train_ds = CellSegDataset(\n            data_dir=train_img_dir, ann_dir=train_ann_dir, split="train"\n        )\n\n        val_ds = CellSegDataset(data_dir=val_img_dir, ann_dir=val_ann_dir, split="val")\n\n        print(\n            f"Loaded {len(train_ds)} training samples and {len(val_ds)} validation samples."\n        )\n\n        # Run the main function\n        predicted_masks = cell_segmentation(train_ds, val_ds)\n\n        print(f"\\nSuccessfully generated {len(predicted_masks)} prediction masks.")\n        if predicted_masks:\n            print(f"Shape of the first mask: {predicted_masks[0].shape}")\n            print(f"Data type of the first mask: {predicted_masks[0].dtype}")\n            print(\n                f"Unique instance IDs in first mask: {len(np.unique(predicted_masks[0])) - 1}"\n            )\n\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f"An error occurred during the main execution: {e}")\n\n```\n\n# Execution output:\n```\n✅ Added /work to sys.path\nWorking dir: /\n✅ Added /work to sys.path\nWorking dir: /\n  program_path: /work/program.py (exists: True)\n[INFO] Loading p