ained for only two epochs at a 640px resolution, despite the code specifying a much larger `yolov8l-seg` model, 80 epochs, and a 1024px resolution. This failure to execute the intended configuration resulted in poor performance. My solution reinstates the original, more powerful training strategy. I will use the `yolov8l-seg` model, train for 80 epochs with early stopping, and use an image size of 1024px to capture fine details in the microscopy images. This ensures the model has sufficient capacity and training time to learn complex cell boundaries, directly addressing the under-training issue observed previously.\nResults: The YOLOv8n-seg model was successfully trained for 2 epochs on the provided cell segmentation dataset. The resulting model achieved a final SEG score of 0.231 on the validation set. The performance was inconsistent across the two validation sequences, likely due to the very short training duration.\nValidation Metric: 0.231\nAnalysis: The YOLOv8n-seg model was successfully trained for 2 epochs on the provided cell segmentation dataset. The resulting model achieved a final SEG score of 0.231 on the validation set. The performance was inconsistent across the two validation sequences, likely due to the very short training duration.\n\n-------------------------------\nDesign: My proposed solution is to significantly scale up the model\'s capacity and training duration to better capture the intricate details of cell structures. The current `yolov8s-seg` model will be replaced with the much larger `yolov8l-seg` variant, which has more parameters and a greater ability to learn complex features. To allow this more powerful model to fully converge, the maximum number of training epochs will be doubled from 40 to 80, while retaining an early stopping mechanism to prevent overfitting. This combined change directly addresses the previous model\'s likely state of under-training and limited capacity, which is crucial for achieving high accuracy on high-resolu