revious solution used a small `yolov8s-seg` model and a limited training schedule of 40 epochs, which, according to past results, resulted in an undertrained model. To significantly improve performance, I will replace it with the much larger `yolov8l-seg` model, which has a greater capacity to learn the complex and fine-grained features of cell boundaries. To allow this more powerful model to fully converge, I will also double the maximum number of training epochs to 80, while retaining an early stopping mechanism to prevent overfitting. This combination will directly address the underfitting and capacity limitations of the prior approach.\nResults: The baseline YOLOv8n-seg model was successfully trained for two epochs, achieving a final SEG score of 0.231. The performance was notably inconsistent across the two validation sequences, with scores of 0.413 and 0.048 respectively, suggesting the model did not generalize well under the limited training schedule.\nValidation Metric: 0.2307617376731882\nAnalysis: The baseline YOLOv8n-seg model was successfully trained for two epochs, achieving a final SEG score of 0.231. The performance was notably inconsistent across the two validation sequences, with scores of 0.413 and 0.048 respectively, suggesting the model did not generalize well under the limited training schedule.\n\n-------------------------------\nDesign: The previous solution employed a relatively small `yolov8s-seg` model, which may lack the capacity to fully capture the intricate and subtle boundaries of cells in high-resolution microscopy images. To address this, I will substitute it with the much larger and more powerful `yolov8l-seg` model. This larger architecture has significantly more parameters, enabling it to learn more complex features and improve segmentation accuracy. To ensure this more capable model has sufficient time to converge, I will also increase the maximum number of training epochs from 40 to 80, while retaining the early stopping mechani